  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What does Mohr's circle show?",
      "a": "It shows the normal and shear stress on every plane through a point. Each point on the circle is one plane; turning the plane by an angle moves you twice that angle around the circle.",
      "asks": [
        "how do I draw Mohr's circle",
        "what is Mohr circle used for"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do I plot the diameter of the circle?",
      "a": "Plot point X at sigma x and tau xy, and point Y at sigma y and minus tau xy. The line joining them is a diameter, and its middle is the centre.",
      "asks": [
        "Mohr circle points X and Y"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What are principal stresses?",
      "a": "They are the largest and smallest normal stresses at a point, acting on planes where the shear stress is zero. On Mohr's circle they are where it crosses the normal stress axis.",
      "asks": [
        "principal stress definition"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do I find the maximum shear stress?",
      "a": "In plane stress, the maximum in-plane shear stress equals the radius of Mohr's circle. It acts on planes at 45 degrees to the principal planes.",
      "asks": [
        "max shear stress from Mohr circle"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why are angles doubled on Mohr's circle?",
      "a": "The stress transformation equations depend on two theta, so rotating the real plane by theta moves the point by two theta around the circle.",
      "asks": [
        "double angle Mohr circle"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "On Mohr's circle, the centre is at the average normal stress and the radius equals the maximum in-plane shear stress.",
    "Principal planes carry zero shear stress and are 90 degrees apart."
  ],
